// Minimal reproduction for the `rt_sched_spawn` backpressure deadlock
// (docs/concurrency-decision.md, "Phase 3.5"): a tight `spawn` loop, run as
// green thread 0 (the top level, per rt_run_program) on the only carrier,
// pushing past the shared queue's capacity.
//
// Before the fix, `squeue_push` blocked a FULL queue with a real
// `pthread_cond_wait` no matter who called it -- correct for an ordinary OS
// thread (some OTHER carrier is always free to drain and signal `not_full`),
// wrong the instant the caller is a green thread running on the ONLY
// carrier that could ever drain it: that carrier is now blocked waiting on
// itself, forever. Run with `LANG_NUM_CARRIERS=1` and a small
// `LANG_GLOBAL_QUEUE_CAP` (both set by runtime/spawn_backpressure_test.sh)
// so N spawns past the cap is reached almost immediately rather than only
// past the production default of a million.
//
// After the fix, the same scenario completes and prints N: `squeue_push`
// parks the calling green thread instead of blocking the carrier when the
// caller is one, exactly the way `Chan.send`/`recv` already park instead of
// blocking (rt.c) -- freeing the carrier to go drain the very queue this
// green thread is waiting on.

const int N = 300;

void worker(int id, Chan<int> out) {
    send(out, id);
}

Chan<int> results = Chan<int>(N);

int i = 0;
while (i < N) {
    spawn worker(i, results);
    i = i + 1;
}

int got = 0;
int sum = 0;
while (got < N) {
    sum = sum + recv(results);
    got = got + 1;
}
// N consecutive integers 0..N-1 sum to N*(N-1)/2 -- printed as a plain
// number so the test script can check it exactly, the same "sum, not an
// order-dependent value" discipline corpus/core's own 025-spawn.src uses.
print(sum);
